("v7192", "scalar", "Skip to the story\nA\nAaron Alderman\nThe mission\nThoughts\nMy Mind\n\nAARON ALDERMAN / A PERSONAL EXPEDITION\n\nInside the mind of a\nmodern-day\nauthor.\nANOTHER SIDE\n\nWords, worlds, and the possibilities between the lines.\n\nLink to this world ↗\n\nBEGIN WITH\n\nQuestions\n\nWhat do I owe the people who enter my stories?\n\n↗\nThreads\n\nFollow attention, story and revision.\n\n↗\nAtlas / Map\n\nExplore attention, possibility and story.\n\n↗\nThere’s more beneath the surface.\nExplore the systems, applications and tools\n↓\n\nTHE WORK THROUGH AN AUTHOR’S EYES\n\nWords make worlds. Worlds teach us what to notice.\n\nSee all projects ↓\n\nFOUNDATION & PLATFORM\n\nGive an imagined world a coherent frame.\n\nTemple and Sourcery provide an explicit way to describe behaviour and compose its parts. I’m interested in how that can support a world whose choices and changes remain understandable.\n\nExecution\nFoundation\n\nTemple\n\nAccountable execution. Explicit rules, checked changes and a history you can inspect.\n\nA world needs a dependable account of how its rules act.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nPlatform\nBuilt on Temple\n\nSourcery\n\nAuthor meaning and compose systems, built on Temple.\n\nCompose the pieces of an interactive world in a common language.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nAPPLICATIONS & PROGRAMMES\n\nFollow meaning into consequences.\n\nA story asks people to believe, notice and choose. These examples help me examine an interpretation, an encounter or the evidence behind a real-world claim.\n\nCreation\nLive example\n\nEncounter\n\nInspect meaning. Follow a change.\n\nLook closely at meaning and what a revision changes.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nSimulation\nEncounter prototype\n\nThe Field\n\nPractise attention, choice and responsibility through an encounter and its possible consequences.\n\nExplore a situation through choices, relationships and uncertainty.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nKnowledge\nApplication\n\nClaimipedia\n\nClaims, evidence and disagreement, with their sources and history still attached.\n\nDistinguish an invented world from a claim about the real one.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nTAKING SHAPE\n\nLet a story find its form.\n\nWorlds, scenes and creative tools in development: room for more than one route through a story, more than one medium, and consequences that continue beyond a single scene.\n\nCreation\nIn progress\n\nWorldwright\n\nCompose stories with more than one way through.\n\nThe developing storytelling platform at the centre of this perspective.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nCreation\nIn progress\n\nCreator Studio\n\nShape an idea as a story, a film or an interactive experience.\n\nLet an idea move between prose, images, film and interaction.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nSimulation\nIn progress\n\nSimulation\n\nExplore how systems behave, from models of possible worlds to an industrial chain from mine to port.\n\nAsk what a mechanism makes possible beyond the immediate scene.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\nWorld Modelling\nFoundry\n\nTOOLS & WORKBENCHES\n\nMake space for the unfinished work.\n\nCreative work also needs a place, a next step and an honest view of what depends on what.\n\nCoordination\nTooling\n\nWorkgarden\n\nGive work a place and a next step.\n\nGive the unfinished scene a place to wait and a next step.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\n\nCoordination\nTooling\n\nHarborboard\n\nPlan work. See what depends on what.\n\nKeep the structure of a larger creative undertaking in view.\n\nExplore\n↗\nInspect\nAll projects · the complete catalogue\n\nCurrent theme: author.\n\nAaron Alderman\n\nA few things I’ve built. A few things I’m still trying to understand.\n\nGitHub ↗\nLinkedIn ↗\nBuilt with Worldwright.io ↗\nAssurance\nLicence ↗\nChristian stewardship covenant ↗")
